Here's a simple HTML and JavaScript code to transform the mock-up into a colorful website with two real jokes:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
            margin: 20px;
        }
        .container {
            border: 2px solid black;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: white;
            width: 600px;
            margin: auto;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        h1 {
            color: #333;
        }
        p {
            color: #555;
            font-size: 1.2em;
        }
        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 1em;
            cursor: pointer;
            border-radius: 5px;
            transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #ccc;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
        <div id="joke1">
            <p>[Really funny joke 1]</p>
            <button onclick="revealPunchline()">Push to reveal punchline</button>
        </div>
        <div id="joke2" style="display:none;">
            <p>[Same, but joke 2]</p>
            <button onclick="revealPunchline()">Push to reveal punchline</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        function revealPunchline() {
            var jokeDiv = document.getElementById('joke1');
            if (jokeDiv.style.display === 'none') {
                jokeDiv.style.display = 'block';
            } else {
                jokeDiv.style.display = 'none';
            }
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a basic webpage with two jokes. The jokes are initially hidden and revealed when the "Push to reveal punchline" buttons are clicked. The

Here's a simple HTML/JavaScript example to create an interactive "Joke Website" as depicted in your mock-up:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }
        .joke-container {
            border: 2px solid blue;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: white;
            width: 300px;
            text-align: center;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        }
        h1 {
            color: blue;
        }
        button {
            margin-top: 20px;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            cursor: pointer;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
            background-color: #007BFF;
            color: white;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="joke-container">
        <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
        <p id="joke">[Funny Joke]</p>
        <button onclick="revealPunchline()">Push to reveal punchline</button>
    </div>

    <script>
        function revealPunchline() {
            document.getElementById('joke').innerText = 'Why don\'t scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!';
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple webpage with a centered joke container that includes a heading, a joke placeholder, and a button to reveal the punchline. The joke is replaced with a real one when the button is clicked. Adjust the CSS styles to match the colors you want for your website.
